## Context
The title claims "A Benchmark and Research Programme". The claim is earned —
frozen task, public gold reference (177-plant register, CC BY 4.0, with
provenance), computable metric (row-level F1 + LP matcher + quality
dimensions), multi-system comparison — but two reviewer mines remain:
(1) contamination is structural (the reference derives from public sources
in every model's training set; the paper measures this as the parametric
ceiling rather than fighting it); (2) N=1 task instance (one country, one
subsector, one reference date) — a benchmark task, not a suite.

Decision (author, 2026-06-11): keep "Benchmark" in the title; pre-empt the
referee with one explicit scoping sentence in the introduction instead of
weakening the title to "testbed"/"evaluation".

## Relevant files
- `slides/manuscript/main.tex` — introduction (around the key-results
  statement placed by 0537, which already says "new computable benchmark")

## Actions
1. Add one sentence to the introduction, near the key-results statement,
   defining the precise sense claimed, e.g.: "We use \emph{benchmark} in the
   narrow sense of a frozen task definition, a public gold reference, and a
   computable metric; we claim no suite or leaderboard." Adapt wording to the
   surrounding prose register (hedged, plain verbs).
2. Check the sentence does not duplicate an existing scoping statement
   (0537's reorg may have added one); if equivalent prose already exists,
   waive with reason in log.
3. Optionally one clause acknowledging that contamination is measured, not
   prevented (the parametric-ceiling design) — only if the intro does not
   already say this nearby.

